INSERT OR REPLACE INTO LocalizedText (Tag, Language, Text)
VALUES


("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_EGYPT_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_6", "en_US", "Egypt's written history begins around 5000 years ago with Menes, which aptly means “he who endures.” According to myth, Menes accepted the crown directly from the falcon-headed god Horus. This was still not the centralized Old Kingdom of pyramids and sphinxes – for that, one has to advance another half-century to the reign of Djoser, the likely founder of the Old Kingdom. Djoser (or more likely his vizier Imhotep) piled stone mastaba tombs one upon another, beginning the tradition of pyramid-building, which would see its height during the reign of later pharaohs. Khufu was paramount among these rulers as he was the builder of the Great Pyramid at Giza. The Old Kingdom fell around 2100 BC, for reasons that may have been political, environmental, or both. Regardless, a period of de-centralization followed."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_EGYPT_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_8", "en_US", "The most jarring split may have come during the invasion of the Hyksos, a people from the Near East who established a foreign dynasty during the Middle Kingdom. But within a few generations, the Hyksos had adopted Egyptian culture to the point that the invaders became indistinguishable from the invaded. During the New Kingdom (1550-1070 BC), Egypt's might peaked. At this point, pyramid-building had long since passed, but the massive architecture of Ramses eclipsed these structures. The statues of the god-king in Thebes and Memphis displayed the magnificence and wealth of the time, which continued into the reign of Ramses' descendant, the pharaoh-queen Hatshepsut. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_EGYPT_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_9", "en_US", "This was the last golden age of a kingdom that had shone brightly for a long time. In later years, powers such as Assyria, Persia, and eventually Greece and Rome, conquered Egypt. However, the region was never a backwater and became one of the most prized possessions for any Empire, largely owing to its rich grain sources. When Rome took the region, its riches flowed directly into imperial coffers, making figures like Augustus wealthy in ways they could not achieve from European land holdings alone. Around 640 AD, the Rashidun Caliphate made the final end to ancient Egypt, although centuries of Roman, Greek, and Byzantine rule had already altered it. Today, Egypt remains one of the most iconic empires of the ancient world, its immense ruins a testament to the legacy and ingenuity of its people. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_GREECE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_4", "en_US", "However, this was not a period of peace. Athens and other city-states fought wars against threats both foreign and domestic. Sparta and Athens, with their radically different outlooks on life and the state, warred for dominance while Carthage and other neighbors posed a constant threat. By the fourth century BC, the Greek peninsula was exhausted by constant battle. The northern power of Macedon was not. Alexander the Great rose from Macedonia to lead a united Greece on a world-spanning conquest that extended the empire's borders to India and beyond (but not westward into nascent Rome). After he divided his realm between his generals, however, this united Greece quickly fell into disunity and its successor states soon fell to other powers. The mainland was subsumed in 146 BC by its western neighbor, Rome."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_HAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the fertile river valleys of the eastern coast of the Asian continent, a new power emerged around 1000 BC. Our first clear glimpse of China was with the Zhou Dynasty: a series of powerful military states. Each of these states vied to become the hegemon, the protector of the emperor who was lord of the symbolically powerful yet militarily weak Zhou state. Maintaining imperial authority over a military power was a tricky balance – the final era of this dynasty wasn’t called “Warring States” for no reason. Different states employed different strategies: compromising, growing large and ethnically diverse (Chu), or becoming rigid and martial (Qin). As any who has heard the name Qin Shi Huang could guess, the Qin won out, largely through military mobilization and a basic but effective strategy of making alliances with distant powers."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_HAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "However, the Qin were poor protectors. They overthrew the Zhou emperor and embarked on a forward-thinking, martial expansion that frightened other Chinese states. Despite Qin Shi Huang’s claims that he would be a universal, timeless monarch, death eventually came for him. The Qin had claimed they were the new, martial future for Chinese civilization, but the other Chinese states put a stop to that. A new era of warring states emerged, split between the Chu state and the Han state. Under the emperor Gaozu, the Han emerged from the Qin’s ashes in 202 BC and sought to combine Qin efficiency with Zhou legitimacy. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_HAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_7", "en_US", "Conflict broke out during a series of rebellions in 184 AD that would end with the Han Dynasty’s shattering fall, a period epitomized in the “Romance of the Three Kingdoms.” During this time, rival factions controlled different sections of the former kingdom, notably the ruthless Cao Cao, Sun Quan, and Liu Bei. Cao Cao's son, Cao Pi gained control of the former Han emperor and forced the emperor to abdicate the throne, ending the dynasty. Han lasted for over 400 years, from 202 BC until 220 AD."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_KHMER_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_7", "en_US", "Jayavarman II was the god-king who broke free. At the time, Khmer was a satellite of the Shailendra Dynasty of Java, but in 802 AD Jayavarman crowned himself king of Kambuja (Cambodia) and initiated a campaign of expansion. Jayavarman’s conquests extended across mainland Southeast Asia and established a permanent Khmer presence that has lasted to the present (occasional Siamese dominion notwithstanding). "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_MAURYA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "Enter Alexander the Great, a product of similarly shifting city-states, who marched across this fractured landscape in 327 BC and encountered the Nanda Empire. Alexander’s troops threatened to mutiny in the face of such a powerful force so far from home, which precipitated Alexander’s march back west. India seemed unconquerable. However, a single generation later, Nanda was fading. Chandragupta Maurya, a king from the Indus region, decided it was time to confront the fading Nanda kings and make a play for power."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_MAYA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Mesoamerica has one of the longest-known histories in the New World. The earliest evidence for settlements comes from nearly 10,000 years ago, but most details are lost to time. The Olmec (not their word) is one of the most visible of these ancient cultures (existing from around 1500-400 BC), and they seem to display the classic Mesoamerican features: the ball game, religion centered around blood and bloodletting, and colossal stone art."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_MAYA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "The formation of Maya culture extends from 2000 BC to the present, and the term “Maya” encompasses everything Preclassic to the Zapatistas of today. In Civilization, we refer to the Classic Maya, between 250 BC and 900 AD, when some of the greatest structures in Mesoamerica were built."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_MAYA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_4", "en_US", "By 200 BC, the Maya were already living in complex cities, with impressive stone artistry and the iconic Maya hallmarks – the script and the calendar. Early studies posited that the Maya were a peaceful, priestly society. The American New Age movement had a notion that Maya cities were merely temples and sites for astronomy that rose out of the jungle. This is profoundly incorrect; LIDAR and earlier studies have revealed dense networks of canals and cultivated zones that extended around cities. Furthermore, cities were not at all peaceful. City-states were frequently at war, and human sacrifice was common. Still, the fascination between pseudoarchaeology, New Age spirituality, and the Maya remains."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_MAYA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_7", "en_US", "Around 800 AD, things started to fall apart. The environment might have been a part of it. Maya cities rarely depended on rivers as they were built atop the large natural aquifer of the Yucatan peninsula, which was useful for preventing the spread of water-borne disease and provided ubiquitous sources of fresh water across the forest but was also vulnerable to changing climates. During a drought, a river might slow to a trickle, but some form of stream will remain. On the other hand, when underground sources of water dry up, they dry up everywhere. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_MISSISSIPPIAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the late first millennium AD and the early second, the Mississippian society stretched from Louisiana to Michigan, and from east Texas to the South Carolina Atlantic. These people were called Mississippians largely on account of the river that ran through their center. Archaeologically speaking, they were a culture, meaning they had commonalities in land use, architecture, pottery, and other tools. However, since the Mississippians lacked a system of writing, our understanding of their culture is missing details: whether or not the group was united or divided, the names of its famous people, and the specific meanings of its symbols and legends. The archaeological imprint of violence (or the lack of it), the movement of goods, and shared iconography suggest political structures and reveal trade routes. Still, several questions remain: were city-states politically unified? Did they all speak the same language? Does the evidence for large public ball games in North America point to the same institution from Maya sites? Archaeologists can make educated guesses, but they are only educated guesses."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_MISSISSIPPIAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "The Mississippians grew out of what archaeologists call the late Woodland period (1000 BC-1000 AD). During this time, people in this region steadily increased the use of stone and bone technologies, and invented tools like blowguns and the bow (they had previously used spear-like atlatls). Over time, hunting groups developed pottery and agriculture (gourds and maize), though hunted deer remained important. Copper mining in the region began as early as 3000 BC. Most copperworking involved manually bending the metal until it became warm enough to be pliable, and then riveting it to other sheets. Copper and brass were mostly used for ornamentation, although some tools and weapons were fashioned out of copper."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_MISSISSIPPIAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_4", "en_US", "Around 800 AD, increasing sedentarism consolidated into permanent cities, the largest of which was Cahokia. Prior interpretations of Cahokia placed it at the center of trade routes directly controlled by rulers. But later evidence points to its role as the center of a more indirect empire, what archaeologists call a “paramount chiefdom,” in which rulers lead via influence and networks, rather than direct administration. A paramount chiefdom consists of a series of noble families; a chief comes from the upper levels of those families. This system of rule could be complicated by succession laws or election – it’s impossible to tell. Further, it seems that Mississippian systems were cyclical; nobles from different cities rose to power at different times. In short, this was a region with a high degree of political instability and social fluidity. Cahokian society revolved around class divisions and the appropriation of surplus production, though people who lived lower on the social scale probably lived communally."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_PERSIA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "“Persia” is the Greek term for the area often referred to as Iran. The region has been embedded in nearly all of Old World antiquity: neighboring Greece, India, China, and northern Europe. The Sumerians overlapped Iran from Mesopotamia, and the Elamite civilization arose in the region as early as 6,000 years ago. However, the story about a recognizable Iranian people begins with the fall of the great Mesopotamian powers – namely Assyria. Ashurbanipal’s final loss of the region came at the hands of the Medes, Persian pastoral nomads who had lived alongside the Elamites since around 1000 BC."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_PERSIA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "The scope and scale of the Median Empire remain contested – Greek sources point to a well-organized realm, but this image lacks corroboration elsewhere. Regardless, in 553 BC, Cyrus (eventually called Cyrus the Great) revolted against the Medes. After he won, he expanded the Persian Empire and defeated the Lydians (led by the famously wealthy King Croesus) and Babylon. In the latter conflict, Cyrus entered Hebrew history and is credited with freeing Jews from servitude before rebuilding Jerusalem."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_PERSIA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_8", "en_US", "Ironically, in 330 BC, the Achaemenids fell to a Greek invasion led by another “Great” – Alexander. After Alexander’s death, the Seleucid Empire (founded by Alexander’s general) established a Greek kingdom in Persia until the Parthians re-established Iranian dominance. Of course, Iran exists today, and Persian influence extends beyond Iran; it had a profound impact on the Abbasids, the Mughals, and most other nations in the area."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_ROME_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Rome’s history spans centuries; the most generous accounts place Rome's foundations in 753 BC. The volume of material written by, on, and about Rome is staggering. Any brief attempt to capture the complexity of Roman history, its twists and turns, and its varied forms of government will be insufficient."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_COLOSSUS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "The Colossus did not stand for long. In 226 BC, just 54 years after its construction, an earthquake toppled it. When the Rhodians heard from the Oracle at Delphi that they had offended Helios, they left it in ruins, though its massive scale continued to attract tourists. In time, the ruins were taken by conquering Arab forces in 653 AD and sold to a merchant from Syria."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_DUR_SHARRUKIN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Dur-Sharrukin is not a singular wonder but the capital city of Assyria under Sargon II, built sometime before 706 BC in the far north of what is now Iraq. It was a massive feat of engineering, constructed with limited materials and requiring the cultivation of surrounding land. The city’s three square kilometers were enclosed by walls featuring over 150 towers. Inside was a collection of opulent palaces, temples, and a ziggurat, as well as elaborate lamassu and shedu (Assyrian sacred beasts) statues. Dur-Sharrukin also held a sprawling garden composed of trees from every land that Sargon conquered. These gardens became the inspiration for Sennacherib’s gardens at Nineveh, the Hanging Gardens of Babylon, and the Persian pairidaeza."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_EMILE_BELL_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The largest bell in Korea, the Bell of King Seongdeok was cast out of bronze in 771 AD during the Silla period of Korean history. It is a temple bell that was originally installed in Bongdeoksa Temple, where its sound would call monks to prayer and ring out the time of day. A tube at the top of the bell provides a unique reverberation and famously rich sound. This sound is the source of a rather sinister legend – allegedly when the smiths first sought to make a bell for Bongdeoksa, every bell they cast would fail to produce a sound. A monk dreamed that a working bell needed a sacrifice – that a child needed to be cast into the metal. When the bell is struck today, one can easily imagine its ring calls out “emile,” meaning “mother.” This story is likely a later anti-Buddhist invention or a modern ghoulish ghost story."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_GATE_OF_ALL_NATIONS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "By some accounts, Parsa was not a place for permanent habitation, functioning more as a palatial and ritual complex. Regardless, it was a vast place of art and wonder. The Gate of All Nations, built by Xerxes in the 5th century BC, was an extensive entrance supported by four pillars and decorated with bulls and lamassu (sphinxes with the heads of bearded men). The space was designed to impress, to instill insignificance in its viewers as they stared up at the face of Xerxes the Great – before facing its active ruler."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_GREAT_STELE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "Funerary traditions of raising monuments to mark gravesites stem from a broader regional practice in Eastern Africa and date back as far as the third millennium BC. Within this context, the Great Stele marks both the height of Aksum ingenuity and the importance of Aksumite religious practices before the kingdom adopted Christianity."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_HANGING_GARDENS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "At Nineveh, around 700 BC, Sennacherib raised a massive system of waterworks to feed orchards and gardens with water screws that brought irrigation to higher terraces, which is where the “hanging” name comes from. These gardens were filled with trees such as cypress and pine, dates, pears, grapes, and other fruit. In the midst of the arid Iraqi landscape, such a garden would have been a cool, refreshing oasis – as well as a record of conquest."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_MUNDO_PERDIDO_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The Mundo Perdido, the “lost world,” is the central complex at the Maya city of Tikal. Built around 300 AD as a religious and astronomical site, it consists of a tall pyramid with three associated temples and sits in the middle of (what is now) the Guatemalan rainforest. Interestingly, the complex shows influence from the non-Maya Teotihuacan site in Mexico, a testament to the multicultural nature of Mesoamerican politics."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_NALANDA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "As on the Northumberland shore, monks and their wisdom are vulnerable to the swords of raiders. Around 1200 AD, the Persian warlord Muhammad Bakhtiyar Khalji attacked and destroyed Nalanda. According to legend, when the general finally discovered the treasures of Nalanda, he was shocked to discover they were books. He sought those who could tell him the meaning of the texts, but they had all been slaughtered. In the centuries afterward, Nalanda and Buddhism survived in some form in Bihar, but those institutions were never as significant as they had once been."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_PYRAMIDS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The Pyramids (“myr” in Egyptian) are massive tombs raised for kings so ancient that their age is mind-boggling. These structures were raised around 2560 BC, meaning that Cleopatra lived closer to us than she did the construction of the Pyramids. Traditional Egyptian tombs (mastabas) were long, low stone structures atop a deep burial shaft. Pyramids evolved as architects stacked these shapes, making larger and taller mastabas. Later architects, such as the alleged architect of the Great Pyramid, Hemiunu, covered these stacks in a sheath of reflective limestone and placed a capstone on top. Some have claimed the capstone was composed of gold, but that is unlikely as it would have been too great a temptation for thieves."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_PYRAMID_OF_THE_SUN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "The Aztecs gave the great Pyramid of the Sun its name – its Teotihuacan moniker has been lost to time. The space houses elaborate carvings of jaguars and serpents. But during its heyday, around 200 AD, its highest point also contained a temple, rising nearly 250 feet above the Mexican dryland. Even in ruins, it remains impressive."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_TERRACOTTA_ARMY_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Qin Shi Huang is the imperial name of the warlord of Qin, one of the states that protected the Zhou Emperor – until it didn’t. Qin Shi Huang overthrew Zhou, proving that all the ritual trappings in the world were no match for a society organized around militarism. Before his death around 210 BC, Qin Shi Huang commissioned the assembly of an army of clay, for the conquests he would undertake in the spirit world. Nearly 10,000 in number, the soldiers were assembled with lifelike faces, armor, and weapons, including chariots, terracotta horses, and support staff. They were buried along with Qin Shi Huang."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_BUILDING_ANCIENT_WALLS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Evidence of walled cities dates back to the eighth millennium BC when Neolithic inhabitants of Jericho constructed stone fortifications around their settlement. In the ensuing centuries, city walls developed across the globe as both a protective measure and a means of regulating trade. As architecture grew more advanced, these structures took on additional purposes – for instance, the Assyrians and Babylonians erected walls to intimidate as well as defend, boasting of their strength in dedicatory inscriptions."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_BUILDING_HARBOR_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Dating at least as far back as 2500 BC, harbors have been important components of the workings of empire. Within these sheltered bodies of water, ships used for trade and transport are able to moor and transfer goods safe from the winds and storms of the open sea. Though harbors are the bodies of water themselves, whether natural or manmade, the word is used synonymously with “dock” and “port,” coming to mean the general location and structures where ships dock and cargo is handled."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_BUILDING_HARBOR_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "The earliest known harbor used for shipping is the Egyptian site Wadi al-Jarf, located on the Red Sea, that dates to the reign of King Khufu (c. 2585-2555 BC though dates vary). A papyrus logbook found at the site details some of what transpired at this early dock. Limestone from the quarries at Tura was transported by boat through this harbor to eventually make its way to the pyramids at Giza. Though it is unclear what purpose this stone had, it was likely used as a layer of cladding on the outside of the pyramids. Other entries in the logbook record the transport of food and other items, the trajectories of vessels across the kingdom, and the titles of various officials working to keep goods and materials flowing."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_BUILDING_MONUMENT_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "A monument is a place of memory. It is a site – often without another function – built to frame a collective memory of the past, usually to celebrate it (but not always). Monuments provide a template for the public's reading of the past, normally created by those in power. For instance, Trajan’s Column, completed in 113 AD, depicts a benevolent Roman army moving toward a hostile foreign power and the subsequent rebuilding of the conquered lands; the message is that the army acted with goodwill, an important message for a public wary of a Roman military constantly set to seize power. Monuments erected in the 1920s across the American South purported to mourn the loss of the Civil War, but they contained a subtext about the continuing influence of white supremacy in the region. Monuments can also be read against the grain: Bangkok’s Democracy Monument was built by a fascist architect to celebrate a military regime, but these days it is a focal point for pro-democratic rallies. Like narrative, memory alters and changes, and the meanings of monuments today are rarely those the builders intended."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_IMPROVEMENT_HAN_GREAT_WALL_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "With the rise of the Han dynasty in 202 BC, diplomatic negotiations with the nomadic Xiongnu in the north soon erupted into war. In response, the Han strengthened and expanded the Qin walls, leveraging them as offensive structures to great success. Dotted with towers and forts, these walls created positions of strength in contested territories, claiming land and providing a defended route for supplies, communication, and trade. The walls were supplied by civilian and military farms and built largely using the rammed earth technique, in which soil is tightly compacted layer by layer between two vertical boards."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_IMPROVEMENT_ICE_HOUSE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the low humidity of the Persian deserts, the yakhchāl stands as a cone of cool relief. These engineering marvels harnessed the desert air and underground water to create structures so well insulated from the heat that they could store ice even in the desert temperatures. Dating as far back as 400 BC, yakchāls were constructed of a special mortar and featured walls at least two meters thick at their base – all designed to resist heat transfer and keep the interior cool. A shade wall was often constructed nearby to assist with the production and transfer of the ice."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_GREECE_AGOGE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Spartan society has been exalted as a paragon of warrior culture, where all aspects of social life serve the military machine, and this was especially true of the Spartans after the 3rd century BC. Through the agoge, the system of Spartan training and education, boys were segregated from the rest of society and divided into age groups. Each group focused on discipline, athletics, and choral performance, and the boys socialized exclusively with others in their cohort – they lived together and were forbidden from receiving food from other groups."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_GREECE_EKKLESIA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Athens – rightly or wrongly – lays claim to some of the oldest democratic institutions in the world. The ekklesia, the assembly of citizens, was the means through which the city-state reflected democratically on its policies. The assembly was a gathering of all male citizens 18 years of age and older, and it allowed these members to vote on appeals, elections, and other matters of public concern. The ekklesia had been established by 600 BC and functioned (as in many city-states) until the Roman period."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_PERSIA_SATRAPIES_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "The system of satrapies, set by Cyrus the Great in 530 BC, was meant to govern conquests and manage tribute (core Achaemenid Persian lands were exempt). These satrapies were Achaemenid Persian viceroys, responsible for collecting taxes, managing locals, and keeping order. Maintaining the road system was especially important to the highly mobile and responsive Persian Empire. In later years, Alexander the Great adopted the satrap system (indeed, Alexander’s satraps formed splinter kingdoms – e.g. the Ptolemaic Egyptians)."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_PERSIA_ACHAEMENID_EMPIRE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In 550 BC, the Achaemenid Empire arose in Iran, where it became the largest empire the world had seen to that point. While the specific history of the realm is described in another entry, the Achaemenids shone in their administrative innovations. Managing a far-flung empire required a particular balance of centralization and decentralization. If one was too heavy-handed in centralizing power, the wider system could not be properly administered. Alternatively, too light of a touch could allow distant satrapies to spiral off in independence. The Achaemenid Persian emperors carefully accounted for the goods and resources of each region – Egypt for grain, India for gold – and levied taxes designed for each territory called the King's Share, or baziš/bāji. Roads and communications were also vital; ancient observers estimated that a message could reach the furthest outposts in just fifteen days."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_ROME_CIVIS_ROMANUS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "In 90 BC, most of these distinctions were erased by the Lex Julia de civitate, which extended Roman citizenship to loyal states. Later, the Edict of Caracalla in 212 AD extended it further to all free men in the Empire."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_ROME_SENATUS_POPULUSQUE_ROMANUS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "SPQR – Senatus Populusque Romanus – stands for the “Senate and People of Rome,” the whole of the government of the Roman Republic. Note here the word “Rome” – this refers not to a political entity, e.g. the Roman Empire, but rather the city of Rome. Men born in the city itself had particular rights not immediately granted (at least in the Republic and early Empire) to those born in the rest of Italy, much less in far-flung provinces. The label SPQR appears from 80 BC, in the late Republic, to the reign of Constantine in the mid-300s. During the Empire, too, the slogan is notable for its meaninglessness – in an imperial system, both senate and people are spoken for by the emperor. What is notable here, though, is a reversal of a notion of divine kingship: in the latter, power flowed downward from the heavens to the king to the people, whereas in Rome, power derived from the people. Well, the people of the city of Rome, at least."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_TECH_AQ_POTTERY_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The earliest known pottery, dating back 20,000 years to the Paleolithic period, is from China. Other scattered artifacts found from that time include votive figures from Europe and pottery in Jomon Japan and sub-Saharan Africa. The mass production of clay pots was a key moment in the Neolithic period, the era between 7000 BC and 4500 BC just before recorded history and metallurgy, where the fundamentals of ancient society were taking shape. Pottery developed in Mesopotamia spread into Europe in the early parts of the age, though pottery probably arose independently elsewhere in the world around the same time – such as in the Harappan civilization, the Ban Chiang settlements, and South America."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_TECH_AQ_WRITING_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "The very earliest possible writings were a series of dots on images of animals found in cave paintings, which may indicate the seasons they were hunted or act as a system of counting. Elsewhere, systems of symbols used to convey particular information (not fully linked to a full language) emerged in China in the seventh millennium BC and in Europe and the Indus River valley in the sixth. The earliest true writing came about in Sumeria during the fourth millennium BC, where the habit of pressing reeds into clay brought about cuneiform, used by Babylonians and Akkadians in later years. Cuneiform may also have influenced Egyptian hieroglyphs, which arose shortly afterward."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_TECH_AQ_WRITING_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_4", "en_US", "Whereas Mesopotamian writing originated in ledgers and merchants’ accounting books (or in this case, tablets), Chinese writing was linked to divination – markers on oracle bones in the Shang dynasty (circa 1200 BC). In the Americas, writing developed independently in the last few centuries BC, and grew into the complicated Mayan script."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_TECH_AQ_BRONZE_WORKING_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "Bronze is harder than iron but requires sources of copper and tin to make. Securing these two resources was difficult, especially during the collapse of the late second millennium BC, when the Sea Peoples wrought devastation upon the region. As a result, iron became an easier and cheaper source of tools, and the era of the bronze-clad warrior was gone."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_TECH_AQ_WHEEL_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The wheel is a simple invention: a disk featuring a central axis to spin around. Carrying goods on one's back is burdensome, and dragging objects on the ground is destructive. The wheel alleviates both issues, conveying objects with relative ease. The invention is so simple that it was, quite possibly, invented independently by multiple civilizations. While the earliest depictions of the wheel come from Syria around 5000-4000 BC, evidence of wheels in Poland in the 3000s BC and in China from around 2200 BC have also been discovered. The introduction of spokes created wheels that were lighter than the solid disks, allowing for more efficient vehicles and enabling chariots. The spoked wheel was so important to ancient peoples that it was the symbol of the Vedic civilization, a people who swept into India around the 1200s BC – the wheel was later incorporated into Ashoka’s wheel of dharma."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_TECH_AQ_IRON_WORKING_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the Finnish epic Kalevala, the immortal smith Ilmarinen strives to create a metal to aid humans in their efforts to cultivate and tame the land. He believes that adding a drop of honey will sweeten the final product, but he grabs a hornet by mistake, creating iron – a material capable of death as much as service. Iron emerged from trade route disruptions during the late Bronze Age. It was not as strong or as sharp as bronze, but it did not need to be smelted with other metals (bronze is composed of both tin and copper), so it required less complicated trade routes and could tie more people together in commerce, or war. This Iron Age classically extends from the widespread adoption of iron tools until the beginning of written history – from the 11th century BC in the Near East until the reign of Ashoka in South Asia (in China, where writing preceded iron, or in Mesoamerica, where iron was not used, the term is irrelevant)."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_SLINGER_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "A sling is often confused with a slingshot. A slingshot is an elastic band between a forked stick, while a sling is a pouch at the end of a rope. This pouch holds a bullet or stone that can be whipped around and thrown with extreme force. Slings are difficult to find in the archaeological record as soft rope and cloth degrade easily over time. Their relative cheapness also meant they were unlikely to be buried and preserved in royal tombs (unlike swords). Yet slings were in use as early as 10,000 BC, and could be effective weapons in the hands of a trained user. They were significant for the Inca, Egyptians, Greeks, and others around the world. Even today, they are a key improvised weapon for common people protesting against police. They deliver a surprising punch for their small size."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_BALLISTA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The ability to create ever-stronger bows increased the need to train ever-stronger archers. However, after a point, machines proved more powerful than muscles. Powerful bows developed into crossbows, which developed into flattened oxybeles, machine-cranked bows with powerful shots. With additional knowledge of mechanical engineering, these weapons were refined into the ballista, a great bow wound by torsion strings that could fling bolts or stones long distances. Ballistae were used in Rome and Greece beginning around 400 BC, and their ranged attacks were truly devastating. These giant bolt throwers were phased out as warfare grew more diffuse and disorganized, but saw a revival in the Middle Ages alongside larger weapons like trebuchets."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_GALLEY_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The galley dominated the seas of antiquity. Powered by both wind and oars (depending on the weather), these ships were capable of fast movement and were versatile in narrow, treacherous waters. In ship battles, such as the clash at Salamis between Greeks and Persians in 480 BC, galleys could lock together as boarding parties exchanged blows. The vessels were easy to take on and off a beach, meaning they did not require all the infrastructure of a harbor, and they could clear shallow seas where larger ships failed. However, their low draft also created limitations. Their holds were limited compared to the number of oarsmen required for a full crew, and they were vulnerable to rolling swells; they were thus unsuited for ocean travel. Safe intercontinental travel required the development of deep-keeled vessels or innovations such as the Polynesian outrigger and the flexible Norse knarr."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_HORSEMAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "It must have taken great nerve to get off the back of a chariot and onto the back of a horse. The exact timing is fuzzy, but the first real historical evidence of horsemen comes from the Parthians in Mesopotamia during the first millennium BC. These were horse nomads, figures who would haunt Eurasian history until the great khans. They used composite bows and fast horses to great effect against slower and less maneuverable chariots."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_LEGION_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The Roman legion was a unit of Roman troops formed for a particular war – until the Marian Reforms of 107 BC when legions became virtually permanent. Legions included more than one kind of unit and were comprised of mounted soldiers and foot soldiers, generally on the order of a few thousand. Poorer spearmen (hastati) were positioned at the front of the line, while better-armed and armored soldiers (principes and triarii) were placed toward the rear. Soldiers were arranged in the maniple – an elaboration on the phalanx – which left empty spaces within the ranks to allow greater maneuverability and create deadly pockets where over-ambitious enemies might be trapped. These maniples were composed of around two hundred men and commanded by two centurions (each responsible for a hundred soldiers, as the name suggests)."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_MEDJAY_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Medja is a region in Nubia, present-day Sudan, that was always on the frontiers of Egypt. The Egyptians knew its people as Medjay – renowned for their archery skills. Given that the Medjay were just outside Egypt's central control, they could be either a boon or a bane to the pharaohs. These nomadic people from the lands bordering the Red Sea likely spoke a language related to Somali or Oromo. During later years, around 1500 BC, the Egyptian state began incorporating Medjay units into the empire, as did the Kushites to the south. Over time, the meaning of the term “Medjay” drifted from defining the region and came to represent a particular corps of troops, especially those involved in policing – even if those members were ethnically, culturally, and regionally Egyptian. By the 500s BC, the Medjay largely disappeared from historical records."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_ABBASID_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Islam grew from a synthesis of Arab polytheism, Judaism, and Christianity, especially in ascetic movements (hanif). Followers attribute Muhammad with divine revelation around 610 AD. As a leader, he sought to unify fractious tribes under the banner of his new, monotheistic religion. Naturally, this brooked opposition from other cities and other tribes, provoking wars of expansion outside of Arabia and into Byzantine and Persian lands."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_ABBASID_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_5", "en_US", "The Abbasid Revolution (750 AD) was a strategic play. Assassins filtered out across the Umayyad empire, disguised as missionaries and agitators. Descendants of Muhammad’s uncle, Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, spearheaded the movement, using their familial link to convince the Shi’a of their closer ties. To non-Arabs, they promised equal legitimacy for all Muslims regardless of ethnicity, permitting non-Muslims to live in the empire so long as they paid the jizya, a special tax."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_ABBASID_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_9", "en_US", "But expansion proved to be the dynasty’s undoing. Given enough autonomy, the distant provinces saw a chance for complete independence. Accordingly, Persian states in the east, mamluks (Turkish mercenaries) in Egypt, and Arab Morocco all broke off to go their separate ways; by 1000 AD, North Africa had been entirely lost to the Fatimids, a Shi’a dynasty based in Egypt. The Abbasids had also inherited the Persians’ perennial war with the Byzantines – Byzantine/Arab battles over Anatolia were a constant back-and-forth that sapped the coffers of both empires and contributed to constant instability."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_CHOLA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "In an ancient stelae (circa 200 BC), the emperor Ashoka mentions the southern Indian kingdom of Tamilakam. Its “Three Crowned Kings” (Chera, Chola, and Pandya) lived at the very southern tip of the subcontinent, and from them, the Tamil people trace their origins. During this period, myth interspersed with history; according to records, the kingdoms and empires of the south carved rivers and built mountains, and each of the Three Crowned Kings embodied a particular element: the Cheras represented fire, the Cholas the sun, and the Pandyas the moon. The archaeological record reports a basic feudal system. Life in the south involved constant conflict with larger kingdoms to the north as well as rival kingdoms within the southern region – until the beginnings of the Chola dynasty around 700 AD, a time contemporaneous with the rise of Islam to the west and the Khmer to the east."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_HAWAII_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Many think of Hawai’i as a tropical vacation spot. Few consider its history as an independent nation…or as a colony of the United States. Somewhere between 100 and 1000 AD, Polynesians settled on the islands of Hawai’i. Local histories describe a holy man from Tahiti who brought a new order to Hawai’i, establishing a class system of chiefs, nobles, priests, commoners, and the lowest grade (kaua), all divided by a system of kapu (taboo). Thriving in this system involved mana. While the term has become commonplace in fantasy games, the word is Hawaiian in origin. It describes a spiritual energy and healing power that exists within the universe. Sacred people and sites have concretions of mana, especially chiefs or those of chiefly bloodlines. Mana is also inherent to acts like sex, violence, storms, etc. Mana and kapu are linked. By correctly observing the rules of kapu, and through the intercession of priests (kahuna), one can expand their mana. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_INCA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The Andes have a long history of settlement. Caral, for instance, is a site comprising around thirty major cities, including massive pyramids and plazas. Caral arrived at an astoundingly early date – as early as 3700 BC. Its pyramids likely date to around the same time as the Great Pyramid of Giza (in fact, Caral appears to predate Giza by around 100 years). The Andes have been home to large-scale states and monumental architecture for millennia."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_INCA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_4", "en_US", "The Inca Empire rose in the 12th century AD in Cusco, and their history before that remains shrouded in myth. Under Pachacuti (1438-1471), the empire began a campaign of expansion and consolidation, subjugating conquered people through forceful resettling. Conquered people were conscripted into forced labor or drafted as warriors, fueling the mountain war machine."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_MING_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Between 1368-1644 AD, Ming China was the richest and most powerful country of its time. The opulent stories about Florence, Renaissance Italy, or Shakespeare-era London pale in comparison. In the eras to follow, colonization was largely an attempt to gain access to China. Yet the Ming grew complacent. Peace and stability led to stagnation. By the end of the empire, the Europeans had gained the upper hand on the world stage."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_MONGOLIA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "From 1206-1368 AD, the Mongol Empire was the largest contiguous empire in history. While the sun didn't set upon the British Empire, its possessions were divided by water and other territories. The empire built by the great Khans, on the other hand, was a single entity, stretching from Korea to Europe, from the tundra to Southeast Asia. Moreover, the Mongol Empire was designed to be a multi-ethnic state, with relative freedom of religion, a philosophy of free movement, and a lively exchange of ideas. That is, once its people surrendered to the horse lords."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_NORMAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "In 793 AD, Norse raiders discovered the riches of Christian Europe. Vikings set out from ports in Norway and Denmark and began plundering settlements along the rivers and coasts. But the Norse were good for more than burning monasteries: they were explorers, merchants, artisans, and farmers as well as warriors. In many places, especially late in the period, they settled down where they fought. Rollo (Hrólfr) was a perfect case in point. In 911, this Viking leader threatened West Francia. Charles the Simple (named for his honesty, not his intellect) gave Rollo land in exchange for the Norseman’s baptism and pledge of fealty, thus creating the Duchy of Normandy. In fact, the English word Norman comes from the French uptake of the Norse word “norrmaðr,” meaning “Northman.”"),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_SPAIN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Hispania was the name of the Roman province at the end of the Iberian peninsula, which Augustus Caesar fully incorporated into the Empire. As the Roman Empire collapsed under the weight of barbarians, so did Hispania. Gothic tribes – Germanic peoples from the Baltic region – moved into Spain, setting up monarchies in much of the Mediterranean, which quickly became Catholic. These largely fell to expanding Muslim powers, namely the Umayyad Caliphate in 711 AD. Spain’s Muslim rule lasted for many centuries, but it was almost constantly at war with the Gothic kingdoms who were eventually pushed back to the northern shore."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_BRIHADEESWARAR_TEMPLE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Southern India is home to groups that are ethnically and linguistically distinct from the rest of the country – primarily the Tamil people. Though Tamils follow Hinduism, they developed unique traditions and achieved a period of international fame and might during the Chola Dynasty in the first millennium AD (and part of the second). Brihadeeswarar is a testament to Tamil's rich culture, wealth, and strength. The “Meru (sacred mountain of Hindu myth) of the South,” the temple sprawls across a large complex designed for perambulation by pilgrims. At its center is the vimana – the pyramid-shaped tower adorned with carvings and limited to Hindu priests. While many temples were elaborately painted, it is a matter of contemporary debate whether or not the vimana at Brihadeeswarar was (or should be) painted. The tower is currently a sandy orange stone – impressive, regardless of adornments."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_ERDENE_ZUU_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Mongolia has a long and complex history with religion. Early on, the Mongol Empire's religion was a form of shamanism (where an individual’s spirit communicates directly with the divine), holding the god Tengri at the height of an axis of power. Buddhism, especially Tibetan Buddhism, has impacted the region since its introduction only a few years after the life of the Buddha (about 500 BC). But Buddhism's popularity fluctuated with shamanism, flourishing when Mongols were established in settled cities and declining when the Mongols were scattered across the steppe."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_SHWEDAGON_ZEDI_DAW_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Shwedagon Zedi Daw (the Golden Dagon Pagoda), also simply known as Shwedagon Phaya, is a gold-covered stupa that rises 59 feet above the skyline of Yangon, Burma. It reportedly contain relics of former Buddhas: the staff of Kakusandha, the water filter of Konagamana, the robe of Kassapa, and strands of hair from Gautama (the Buddha we normally think of). According to tradition, the stupa was built during the lifetime of the Buddha, nearly 2,500 years ago, although there is no record of the stupa until the 14th century AD. It is the most important site in Burmese Buddhism and is significant in Theravada Buddhism (mainland Southeast Asia and Sri Lanka)."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_BUILDING_SAWMILL_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In a saw pit, men cut logs into planks. This is a long, laborious process made infinitely easier by the application of early machinery, which began in Byzantium in the early first millennium AD. Running water could turn a wheel – via connecting rods or a gear train – to move a stone or wooden saw that could cut through logs without human labor. Other forms of mills existed, namely those powered by wind, animals, or even human labor."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_EX_ABBASID_ROUND_CITY_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Some would say city planning is a modern innovation. They are incorrect. Grid cities existed as far back as 2600 BC in Harappa, in the Indus River Valley, and across Classical antiquity. Cities such as Persepolis were monuments to the learning, forward-thinking, and prestige of the builder. The Abbasids were no different."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_EX_ABBASID_ROUND_CITY_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Thousands of years later, Baghdad, “god’s gift,” was a monumentally planned city. Around 765 AD, Caliph al-Mansūr constructed the round city, which contained the residence of the Abbasid court, the House of Wisdom, the principal Abbasid mosque, and the Abbasid palace. The city was a testament to the divine right of the caliphs to rule their empire, the Dar al-Islam (the Muslim world), as well as all enlightened peoples. Wave after wave of invasion and destruction, by no less than Genghis Khan, proved otherwise."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_EX_MAJAPAHIT_ALIRAN_KEPERCAYAAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Indonesia is a melting pot of peoples, religions, cultures, and traditions – notably the Indonesian language, Bahasa Indonesia, is considered one of the easiest languages to learn simply because it is designed as a lingua franca, a trade language. Indigenous traditions of Javanese, Balinese, Bugis, Dayak, and a multitude of other peoples blended with waves of Hindu and Buddhist traditions from South Asia, and again later with Chinese religions and eventually Islam following the 12th century AD. This combination of religions – referred to as syncretism (the blending of two religious forms to make a third) or hybridity (the ongoing interchange between multiple religious traditions and their mutual alteration) – is referred to as aliran kepercayaan (“the branches of belief”)."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_EX_MING_DA_MING_LU_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The Great Ming Code, written into its final form in 1397 AD, was implemented by the Hongwu Emperor. More than a system of governance, it was a moral and cultural document, a codification of how to remain in line with the natural and social order. It provided a clear break from the past Mongol-led Yuan Dynasty and birthed a resurgence of a Han Chinese social order. In its attempt to place social and moral life under a legal order, it established the bones of a modern order."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_TECH_EX_METAL_CASTING_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The process of casting metal isn’t new to the early modern period; it took off in the later part of the second millennium BC. The process involves pouring molten metal into molds to form complex shapes that are difficult to achieve via the old process of hammering, heating, and bending. While forged metal is generally stronger than cast metal, casting is easy to mass produce, which has become increasingly important as populations expand."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_TECH_EX_CASTLES_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Castles are traditionally defined as fortified private residences. Historically, the stereotypical castle emerged in the later part of the first millennium AD, and the Norman practice of bringing stone castle technology to England was a key factor in their conquest. In Asia, where feudal systems predominated (e.g. Sengoku Japan), castles also became important features of the political landscape."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_TECH_EX_SHIPBUILDING_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Just as sailing advanced in different ways throughout antiquity, the development of shipbuilding looked different in various places throughout the first millennium and a half AD. Polynesia remains one of the most impressive regions in history for shipbuilding: double-hulled Polynesian catamarans with crab-claw sails enabled people to journey vast distances across the Pacific – to Hawai’i, Rapa Nui, and perhaps beyond. While such voyages took place for thousands of years (Samoa was reached around 800 BC) the expanses of the open ocean presented a serious challenge – for instance, consider the distances between the Cook Islands and Aotearoa (New Zealand) or even Hawai’i."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_SWORDSMAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Hammers and axes are tools. Spears and bows can be used to hunt as much as to kill other humans. But a sword is truly a soldier’s weapon. Around 3000 BC, the sword evolved from large daggers, clearly a weapon of conflict. They could be single- or double-edged, curved (to more easily cut an enemy’s body), or straight (ideal for stabbing). Some had wicked hooks (the Egyptian khopesh) or tremendous blades (the Germanic longsword or Chinese zhanmadao). The most famous swords were those made from steel with a particular hardness – Damascus steel or the famous Japanese katana. Importantly, swords required skill and training. A swordsman was not a farmer with a spear, but a skilled warrior was dangerous with any weapon."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_HEAVY_ARCHER_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "No single weapon separates a regular archer from a more deadly one; the best of the best are set apart through a combination of bow technology and training. Bows rely upon the resistance of a bent piece of material – usually wood – to propel an arrow through the air. Increasing that resistance increases the force of the arrow. Medieval archers maximized their effectiveness through the careful selection of wood (e.g. yew) and the construction of taller bows – ultimately producing the English longbow, a weapon that was cheap to make but required specific technique and could not be fired from horseback. Archers in Korea, India, and Arabia fused horns with another resistant material to the insides of their bows, which also increased the power of their shots; this delivered a smaller bow but it was more complicated to make and vulnerable to moisture. Longbows and composite bows existed as far back as 3000 BC, but their use as formidable forces of war is more modern. These archers required special training – English soldiers described their technique of using the body’s weight to push forward and “bend” the bow, rather than using their arms to draw the string. Such bows could penetrate armor and were not rendered obsolete until the adoption of the cannon."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_CATAPULT_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "If a ballista is a massive bow made for siege warfare, the catapult is the equivalent for a sling. The term “catapult” is fairly general, and the classic image of a bowl mounted on the edge of a long arm to fling rocks, made famous in the early Middle Ages, might more specifically be called an onager or mangonel. The onager relied on potential energy stored in a bent wooden plank, whereas the mangonel used a system of cords for propulsion. The general purpose was the same for both: throw heavy things a long way. The mangonel likely originated in China before the Han Dynasty, and the onager in Rome during the 3rd century AD; both could hurl a rock roughly 400 feet, demolishing walls, ships…and the people inside."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_COG_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the Middle Ages, trade routes dominated by the Hanseatic League crossed the Baltic and North Seas (and cautiously delved into the Atlantic Ocean). Their ship of choice was the cog, a slightly rounded, single-masted oak vessel that first made an appearance around 1000 AD. The cog had a sophisticated rudder and raised area for the crew, meaning its interior could be used effectively for cargo. Cogs took longer to construct than southern vessels but could be crewed by fewer people, leaving even more room for goods. In East Asia, Chinese builders worked on similar flat-bottomed models, though these featured more masts and lacked the distinctive “clinker” (overlapping) style of the cog. Tang and Song vessels moved around East and Southeast Asia – no less treacherous waters than the Atlantic!"),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_CETBANG_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", """Mongol hordes"" call to mind a sweeping mass of horse archers stretched across the Eurasian steppe. However the Mongols – referring to Kublai Khan’s Yuan Dynasty in China – also attempted a takeover of Java in 1293 AD, which set the stage for the rise of the Majapahit Empire. This invasion failed thanks to naval treachery, but Java kept the Mongols' guns after the invaders left. The cetbang was a light cannon with one or two barrels, and was mounted onto a swivel, giving it a remarkably modern appearance – the same year that the Majapahit were using double-barreled swivel cannons, the English and French were still contesting over the superiority of longbows or crossbows."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_MAMLUK_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "With military prowess and success came power and respect – even for someone enslaved. Despite their status as property, Mamluk standing rose above even the general freed population. They seized power in Egypt and Syria in 1250 AD, creating the Mamluk Sultanate that persisted for nearly three hundred years, and they decisively expelled the last Crusaders from the Holy Land."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_CORSAIR_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Corsairs, or pirates, were masters of the seas – independent sailors who traditionally attacked and pillaged other vessels for their personal enrichment. Their presence was historically found in regions heavily reliant on naval trade, as merchant ships and coastal cities made easy targets for quick wealth. The oldest recorded corsairs were the Sea Peoples, who raided the Mediterranean between the 14th-12th centuries BC. The East Asian coastlines also had a long tradition of piracy that targeted the ample Indian Ocean trade routes. And the Golden Age of Piracy boomed in the Caribbean during the 18th century when mostly European sailors broke from their home nations to prey on the young colonies in the New World."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_BUGANDA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Buganda’s story starts with Aksum, an ancient state where Ethiopia is today. Aksum collapsed at the very end of the first millennium AD. As a result, the southern region formed their own empire: the Empire of Kitara (Chwezi). In local lore, the Chwezi were spirits with red skin and bronze weapons, originating somewhere to the north. This depiction recalls the ancient Egyptians, though the local people now attribute a supernatural origin to this group. In their classic origin myths, the powerful but arrogant Chwezi gave the local people of the region all sorts of technological benefits (e.g. bronze) and terrible curses (e.g. smallpox). They left sites such as Bigo – a series of earthworks originally thought to represent a fortification that may actually have been a marketplace surrounded by an anti-elephant wall."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_TECH_MO_STEAM_ENGINE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The principles behind the steam engine have been understood for a long time. In 30 BC, the engineer Vitruvius described a mechanism wherein a hollow bronze ball with two small outflow pipes could be filled with water, and once heated, would rotate rapidly. The mechanism was not very efficient but was used at times as a kind of ""special effect"" to open temple doors. Over a millennium later, Leonardo da Vinci proposed a steam-power-operated cannon. During the 17th and 18th centuries, steam engines became more of a reality, especially as improved metalworking allowed for the creation of parts that fit together seamlessly. In 1807, Robert Fulton made the first commercially successful steamboat, leading to steam power's rise in interest and investments, which eventually took the technology to the rails."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_IMPROVEMENT_CLAY_PIT_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The earliest ceramics emerged nearly 30,000 years ago, in the Paleolithic, although these works seem to be artistic or ritualistic objects and not everyday vessels as we commonly know them. The first evidence for the latter comes from China around 18,000 BC. The firing of clay is a marker of the Neolithic, and often heralds the beginnings of large-scale changes in societies, such as agriculture (except for some regions where pastoralism remained important), the use of smaller and smaller stone blades, and the like."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_AUGUSTUS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Where to begin with Caesar Augustus, about whom massive volumes of text have been written, and to whom nearly every subsequent “great leader” west of the Indus River has aspired to be? He was Caesar from 27 BC until 14 AD, and epitomized Rome at its height. Conceivably, he was less bold and more methodical than his great-uncle and adoptive father, Julius Caesar. However, Augustus arguably accomplished more."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_AUGUSTUS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_7", "en_US", "Nevertheless, Octavian was cautious and ensured that his power plays were legitimated by a senate that was slowly falling under his sway. In 27 BC, Octavian changed his name to Augustus. Another name he chose for himself was “victorious commander”: Imperator. With this title came victories: solidified control over Hispania, the Balkans, and Judea, though he also suffered a major defeat in Germany. Much of Augustus’ power as an individual came from taking personal control over the most lucrative territories – for instance, Egypt belonged to him, not Rome. The profits from that immensely wealthy area flowed into his accounts, as they would for his successors."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_AUGUSTUS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_8", "en_US", "Augustus died in 14 AD, possibly succumbing to his constant ill-health, or possibly murdered by Livia, his wife. The latter is a popular theory, but most historians believe her to be innocent. His last words were “Have I played the part well? Then applaud as I exit.”"),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_CHARLEMAGNE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "In 768 AD, Charlemagne inherited a relatively stable Frankish kingdom and decided to expand it. He aggressively pushed against the pagan Europeans to his east, conquering large stretches of Bavaria and parts of northern Germany and Italy, including lands belonging to the Avars, horse nomads from Central Asia that had moved into Europe. In Spain, Charlemagne also clashed with the rising Umayyad Caliphate. While these Muslims sought to consolidate control over Iberia and wrest it from the Gothic and Basque presence, Charlemagne wished to incorporate the region into his larger empire. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_CONFUCIUS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Confucius had plenty of examples of terrible leaders. As the Shang Dynasty fell apart, a new, loosely-assembled dynasty arose: the Zhou (1046-256 BC). Under this dynasty, individual states led by particular generals held sway over a smaller, more centralized state ruled by the emperor. Imagine a popular person at school surrounded by a crowd of bullies constantly fighting for the claim to be their absolute best friend and protector. Oddly enough, fractured and contentious political situations are often fertile ground for writers and philosophers – just ask Machiavelli. Confucius lived on the eastern edge of China at some point during this war-torn period. As his political world broke down around him, Confucius spent his life advocating for sensible order and good governance. Confucian influence calls on would-be charismatic or forceful rulers to curb their power and work for the common good."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_HATSHEPSUT_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Hatshepsut ruled in the early part of the 15th century BC, during Egypt’s New Kingdom. She is notable for being a queen (though many of her statues depicted her as a man – likely for religious reasons), and for embarking on an aggressive building spree around the capital of Thebes."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_HIMIKO_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Early Japanese history is divided into three distinct periods: the Jomon, the Yayoi, and the Yamato. It is during the latter period that what we recognize as “Japan” takes shape, although it is in the subsequent Heian period that a lot of the recognizable art, literature, and dress come into being – recall that the golden age of samurai, etc., does not come about until the middle of the second millennium AD. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_HIMIKO_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "It was this Yayoi culture – Japanese-speaking migrants from the mainland (meaning Korea, in this case) that Himiko ruled. The Yayoi bridged the Neolithic, Bronze and Iron Ages, and grew quite adept at crafting bronze weapons, tools, and, significantly, mirrors. The Kofun era (300-538 AD) saw this industry expand, as well as the development of a shared cultural heritage with strong Chinese influence from Honshu (the big island of Japan) to Kyushu (a smaller island) to Korea. The era ends with the rise of the Yamato clan and consolidation of Japanese power."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_TRUNG_TRAC_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_4", "en_US", "Over time, the Han reconsolidated and sought expansion. In 111 BC, they conquered Nanyue directly. During this “First Chinese Domination of Vietnam,” the Han embarked on an aggressive campaign to turn Yue “tribes” into good Chinese subjects. This involved aggressive suppression of Vietnamese customs and the imposition of Confucianism, the imperial political system, and Chinese writing. These measures were not popular."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_XERXES_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_5", "en_US", "Xerxes' early brush with destiny came when Darius, preparing to leave for Egypt to quash its rebellion, fell ill and unexpectedly died. In the wake of this death, the question of succession loomed. Should the crown go to Darius’ eldest? Or to Xerxes, the grandson of Cyrus? A debate was arranged to solve this question, and Xerxes won, taking the throne in 486 BC, at the age of 35. He also inherited his father’s wars and immediately took to quelling Egypt, followed by Babylon after that city rebelled. He enacted a brutal vengeance upon Babylon, melting the statues of its gods in punishment. Indeed, Xerxes was staunchly religious (likely adhering to a precursor of Zoroastrianism)."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_RESOURCE_HORSES_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Of all the domesticated animals, only the dog has a closer relationship with humans. For much of history, humans shared a special link with horses. If dogs were our substitute eyes and ears, horses were our legs. Though horses evolved in the New World, they migrated to the Old World two million years ago; their cousins in North America were subsequently wiped out (along with a large number of American megafauna). Horses were familiar to nomads of the Central Asian steppe, where they were first domesticated around 5,000 years ago and used for pulling chariots (ancient horses were likely smaller than present-day horses and could more easily pull chariots than support the full weight of a mounted warrior). These chariots became effective tools of war (as the Egyptians found out) but were replaced by the ultimate Eurasian weapon: the horse archer. While the Mongols are the most famous, the Parthians of around 300 BC were the first recorded mounted archers. Later, as horses spread across the world, other plains-based warrior societies took up the tradition. With the invention of the car, the link between human and horse has waned, though they remain close to many people’s hearts."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_RESOURCE_NITER_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Niter, or saltpeter, is potassium nitrate, a widely abundant salt found in caves, especially in arid places. Its nitrogen content makes it valuable as fertilizer. But it has another popular use: when mixed with sulfur and charcoal, it makes gunpowder. Song Dynasty China (960-1279 AD) was the first place to make use of black powder, constructing powerful bombs, fire arrows, and incendiary devices (as well as fireworks). The process soon caught on and spread elsewhere. By the late 1200s, early cannons were being used against (and later by) the Mongols in the Middle East. By the 1300s, the Turks were experimenting with “fire-lances”: guns. The rest is history; as Mao Zedong wrote, political power comes from the barrel of a gun."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_RESOURCE_PITCH_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Pitch is a versatile, naturally-occurring substance that has been used in shipbuilding since at least 2500 BC. Across the Mediterranean, shipbuilders would fasten together wooden planks with dowel and then fill the seams with pitch to caulk them against any leakage. The Egyptians and Phoenicians were early users of this technique, but we see evidence of similar use beyond the Mediterranean as early as the first millennium BC. Austronesians and China had a vast maritime trade network across Oceania, South Asia, and the Middle East during this period and into the first millennium AD made possible by the shipbuilding innovations of pitch. In the colonial period, pitch was one of the most valuable exports from the forested areas of North America."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_ALEXANDER_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Alexander III, later known as Alexander the Great, was born in 356 BC to Philip II of Macedon and his fourth wife Olympias. Eager and ambitious even as a youth, Alexander’s teenage years hinted at his outsized ambitions. At 13, he wagered his father that he could tame a well-bred but unruly horse. Seeing the horse was afraid of his own shadow, Alexander turned him toward the sun, spoke to him calmly, and successfully mounted and rode the horse. Bucephalus, as he named him, would go on to be one of Alexander’s closest companions throughout his campaign, even warranting a lavish funeral after the horse succumbed to battle wounds. Alexander’s first independent campaign came when he was just 15 years old – he successfully put down a rebellion and founded his first city Alexandropolis."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_ALEXANDER_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "When Philip was murdered at a wedding banquet in 336 BC, Alexander took over the mantle of general in the Greeks’ war against Persia. Nominally, the war’s aim was to enact vengeance on the Persians for sacking Athens over 150 years previously, but to Alexander, it was the first step in conquering Asia. When he crossed over the Hellespont, he is said to have struck his sword into the soil and announced his gratitude to the gods for gifting him Asia. Later, when moving further inland, he stopped at Gordion where a well-known tale told of an old ox-cart tied by a complex knot. It was said that whoever could untie the knot would go on to rule all of Asia. Plutarch tells us there were conflicting reports of how Alexander fulfilled the prophecy: most claimed that he sliced the knot through with his sword, ""solving"" the puzzle and claiming his prize. Whatever the truth, Alexander made his case for King of Asia."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_ALEXANDER_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_6", "en_US", "Alexander’s campaign started with Asia, but at its height, it reached across the Aegean, the Levant, and Egypt, and stretched eastward across modern Iran and Afghanistan to the Hypasis River in northern India. Though Alexander wanted to continue to the Ganges and beyond, his army refused to fight any longer. Turning back, he marched his men along the coast of the Indian Ocean and back to Persia. His desire for conquest encompassed the world, and had fate seen fit to leave him alive, perhaps he would have achieved that goal. Alas, his mortality was proven when he came down with an illness after a drinking party, likely a local strain of malaria or typhus. Convinced he would recover, he made no plans for succession and instead spent time charting a future campaign across North Africa that he never completed. He died in 323 BC at 32 years old, leaving an expansive empire in his wake to be fought over for decades by his subordinates."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_HIMIKO_ALT_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Early Japanese history is divided into three distinct periods: the Jomon, the Yayoi, and the Yamato. It is during the latter period that what we recognize as “Japan” takes shape, although it is in the subsequent Heian period that a lot of the recognizable art, literature, and dress come into being – recall that the golden age of samurai, etc., does not come about until the middle of the second millennium AD. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_HIMIKO_ALT_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "It was this Yayoi culture – Japanese-speaking migrants from the mainland (meaning Korea, in this case) that Himiko ruled. The Yayoi bridged the Neolithic, Bronze and Iron Ages, and grew quite adept at crafting bronze weapons, tools, and, significantly, mirrors. The Kofun era (300-538 AD) saw this industry expand, as well as the development of a shared cultural heritage with strong Chinese influence from Honshu (the big island of Japan) to Kyushu (a smaller island) to Korea. The era ends with the rise of the Yamato clan and consolidation of Japanese power."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_MAUSOLEUM_AT_HALICARNASSUS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "One of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world, the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus was a tomb built c. 353-351 BC for Mausolus, ruler of Caria in western Anatolia. It was a magnificent structure designed and decorated by the most talented Greek architects and sculptors of the day, boasting elaborate reliefs, hundreds of sculptures, and a four-horse chariot at its peak, in which stood statues of Mausolus and his wife (and sister) Artemisia. It is from Mausolus and his tomb that the word “mausoleum” originates. The structure survived well into the Middle Ages, but was destroyed, likely by an earthquake, between the 11th and 15th centuries AD, though fragments remain."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_ASSYRIA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "To begin, we must look at Sumerian history. The Sumerians were a rather mysterious people who appeared at the dawn of cities, possibly from the Indian subcontinent. They built some of the earliest cities in what is today Iraq. Over time, the Akkadians under Sargon rebelled and established an empire over former Sumerian lands, only to falter and collapse around 2150 BC. The centuries following this fall were dark, with city-states (including Ashur) fighting for supremacy."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_ASSYRIA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "Assyria was to rise twice: first in the mid-1300s BC and later, c. 900 BC (for reference, this is younger than Egypt, but far older than Greece; at the tail end of Shang China and the rise of Zhou China). Here, we focus on the later Neo-Assyrian Empire."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_BABYLON_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Babylon is remembered today as a city of beauty, culture, and sometimes decadence. It started though as a small and inconsequential city-state, founded around four thousand years ago. The great king Hammurabi rose to power around 1700 BC and spread Babylonian culture across Southern Mesopotamia in what we refer to as the Old Babylonian Empire. Though immensely impactful thanks to the political, cultural, and scientific developments of Hammurabi and his astronomers, this phase of Babylon’s power was short-lived. After Hammurabi died, the empire quickly collapsed and Babylon retreated to less important status, changing hands over the centuries from one neighbor to another. Its cultural impact, however, remained supreme and maintaining hold over Babylon was a sign of prestige even as the city fought continually for independence."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_BABYLON_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "In 631 BC, when the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal died, his kingdom fell into a period of disarray, and Babylon saw an opportunity. A local man named Nabopolassar rose up in rebellion against the Assyrian general in control of the region and successfully overthrew him. Nabopolassar was crowned king and Babylon was independent once more. Seeing the weakness of Assyria, he pushed forward, eventually allying with the nearby Medes and together taking city by city across Mesopotamia. In 612, Assyria’s capital Nineveh fell to the Medo-Babylonian forces, marking the beginning of the end of the Assyrian Empire. By 605, Nabopolassar had conquered his only rival Egypt and supplanted the old Assyrian lands with what we now refer to as the Neo-Babylonian Empire, or in some places, the Chaldean Empire. Alas, he died before he could enjoy the fruits of his conquest."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_BABYLON_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_6", "en_US", "Nebuchadnezzar died in 562 BC, leaving an unsure succession that devolved into a series of coups. The empire never recovered, and a new threat emerged – Cyrus the soon-to-be Great, leader of the Persians. The last king of Babylon, Nabonidus, did little to prepare for the coming showdown. In 539 BC, after only a single military encounter outside the city, Babylon fell to the Persians. Nabonidus was captured and exiled. Babylon would never again rise as an independent power."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_BABYLON_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_7", "en_US", "Though Babylon fell to Persian rule, it remained a city of glory under Cyrus the Great and was the place of the winter residence of the Achaemenids until their eventual fall. Thanks to Cyrus’ policies of tolerance, the city flourished and remained an economic center through the Persian period and long after it was captured by Alexander the Great in 333 BC. It was not until the seventh century AD that Babylon would begin to decline and by the 13th century, it had become only a small village and lost relevance for centuries. In the modern day, it found new life under Saddam Hussein who, in the Babylonian style of kingship, was determined to rebuild the glory of the city. Though his plans for a new Hanging Gardens never materialized, he did leave a portrait of himself and King Nebuchadnezzar with an inscription that mimicked Nebuchadnezzar’s style and named Hussein as Nebuchadnezzar's son. Today, it is a World Heritage Site and many villages populate the old city’s area."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_IMPROVEMENT_KIRIMAHU_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Like many of the Mesopotamian cultures in Antiquity, Babylonian kings and elites enjoyed the presence of nature for contemplation and relaxation. These pleasure gardens, the kirimāḫu, were typically located outside of the palace but still within the surrounding grounds. Some cities had larger-scale gardens located in the outskirts that functioned more like a wildlife park and botanical garden, the most famous example of which was in Nineveh, the capital of Assyria that fell to the Babylonians in 612 BC. In Babylon’s city center, we know of one specific space called the “Juniper Garden” (kirî burāši) that was situated between two temples and enclosed a library and “House of Deliberation,” among other structures."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_IMPROVEMENT_KIRIMAHU_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "The water required for these gardens was channeled through a complex network of canals that transported water from the Euphrates River. Evidence for these canals dates back to the mid-2nd millennium BC in the Old Babylonian period, showing the advanced engineering skills Babylon developed to support the agricultural needs of its population and later the aesthetic desires of its elites."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_BABYLON_PURATTU_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The Euphrates River, Purattu in Akkadian, flowed through the city of Babylon and, along with the Tigris River, was integral to the irrigation system that supplied the gardens and farms of the surrounding region. Evidence for irrigation in the Euphrates basin dates back to as early as 6000 BC, though the complex systems of transporting and raising water via canals and mechanisms only developed in the region in the first millennium BC."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_BABYLON_AKITU_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "From as early as the third millennium BC, Babylonians celebrated Akitu, a festival or series of festivals, at the beginning of their new year, the start of spring and the sowing season, and at the start of autumn. Babylon would fill with visitors from neighboring towns and cities, and trade flowed amidst the religious activities and general festive atmosphere. Many new harrānu, business partnerships, were formed during Akitu, as it was one of the best opportunities of the year to meet new partners and create new trade ventures."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_BABYLON_AKITU_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "Akitu was celebrated throughout the Mesopotamian valley into the first millennium AD. In the 20th century, it was resurrected as an Assyrian New Year and is celebrated still today across the diaspora of Assyrians, Chaldeans, and Arameans, and within Iraq on April 1st. Individuals wear traditional clothing or dress up as Assyrian royalty, and women create garlands to hang on their homes’ front doors – the “Beard of April” that welcomes spring."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_BABYLON_ENUMA_ELISH_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The great Babylonian creation myth, Enuma Elish, tells the story of how the cosmos came to be, how Marduk fought with the other gods and became the highest, how he made man and the earth, and how Babylon was built for him. Our earliest manuscript was recovered from Nineveh in the 19th century and dates to the ninth century BC. The poem’s composition came a few centuries earlier, most likely during the Second Dynasty of Isin (c. 1155-1026 BC) when Marduk had achieved his position as head of the pantheon. Before Marduk, Enlil was head of the gods and spoke to his followers through Marduk and Ishtar, the other important deity of Babylon. Though she plays only a minor role in Enuma Elish, other poetry gives her a primary place, such as the Descent of Ishtar, the Hymns of Agushaya and Ammī-ditāna, and, of course, the Sumerian Epic of Gilgamesh."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_RILA_MONASTERY_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the 10th century AD, the monk Ivan (or John) of Rila retreated to the Rila Mountains to live as a hermit. He became known for miracles, attracting many followers who flocked to his isolated cave to learn from him and receive his blessing. These visitors set up shelters for themselves, at first temporary, then permanent – the original Rila Monastery. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_CARTHAGE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The Bronze Age collapse in the 12th century BC brought many civilizations around the Mediterranean and Near East to their knees. Climate change, technological imbalances, invasions, and other factors combined to eat away at centralized governments, resulting in the collapse of empires and the dispersal of people from urban centers into smaller rural settlements."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_CARTHAGE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "Around 814 BC, a group of colonists from Tyre arrived at the Gulf of Tunis in modern-day Tunisia. The location was ideal for a new settlement. An easily defendable hill overlooked a harbor that both provided shelter from storms and stood in close proximity to the Strait of Sicily, a bottleneck for Mediterranean trade. The Tyrians quickly began to build their colony, which they named “qrt-ḥdšt” (“new city”). Today, we call it Carthage."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_QUARTER_PUNIC_PORT_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "As a naval economic power, Carthage’s success and survival relied on its port. The remarkable structure consisted of the outer commercial dockyard and the inner cothon for warships. The entire project was excavated from marshland circa 200 BC, requiring the movement of around 235,000 cubic meters of soil. They were built of sandstone ashlar blocks, and were largely concealed from the outside by immense walls; the narrow entrances to the outer and inner harbors could be closed off with huge iron chains. When the Romans conquered Carthage during the Third Punic War, the port was the first part of the city to be destroyed. Yet its footprint remains prominent on the Tunisian coast, one of the few vestiges of Carthaginian glory. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_CARTHAGE_SICILIAN_WARS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The Carthaginians followed the Phoenicians in establishing themselves as the masters of Mediterranean trade routes. But they were rarely unchallenged. Their first major rival were the Greek city-states, another strong naval presence who contested Carthage’s dominance in the region. Their major point of contention was the island of Sicily, which effectively divides the Mediterranean in two halves and was the lynchpin to mastery of the sea in ancient times. The Carthaginians and the Greeks fought a series of wars between 480-306 BC over control of the island, during which territories moved back and forth between the two powers. The Sicilian Wars faded as Rome eclipsed Greece, and the Greek holdings on Sicily became Roman. But the rise of a new competitor for Carthage also sparked a new series of conflicts – the Punic Wars, which began, once again, over Sicily. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_DAI_VIET_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Vietnam, caught between Southeast Asian and East Asian worlds, has a history that is run through with both conflict and interconnection. Its early history is marked by indigenous kingdoms and Han domination, but in 939 AD Ngô Quyền, rebelling against the Southern Han, declared a new era. While Ngô Quyền’s own family would not rule it, and while it would be decades before the name came into use, the foundations of Đại Việt had been laid. For almost a thousand years afterwards, from its capital near present-day Hanoi on the Red River, Đại Việt went through periods of aggressive expansion, but also subjugation and loss. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_DAI_VIET_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "In 1010 AD, Lý Thái Tổ, seeking to establish the glory of the new kingdom, renamed the capital Thăng Long, meaning “rising dragon.” On these rising wings, Đại Việt expanded bureaucratic rule at home but also pushed outward – north into Chinese cities, south into those held by the Cham, and westward to Lao and Khmer lands. At its core, the Đại Việt court was a complex mix, formed by political intrigue between eunuchs and noble families: a regime that prioritized bureaucratic and meritocratic power, and both incorporated ethnic others and asserted imperial control. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_DAI_VIET_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_5", "en_US", "Chinese control lasted roughly two hundred years, and in the rebellion of Lê Lợi in 1418 AD that Đại Việt finally broke free of Chinese control. Wresting control from the Ming via a campaign of guerilla warfare, the new regime brought a fusion between earlier customs and Chinese norms, between tradition and new technologies – such as firearms. It would need them. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_EX_DAI_VIET_CHU_NOM_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Đại Việt had no shortage of choices for writing systems to draw upon for writing Vietnamese, surrounded as they were by Pali, Khmer, Sanskrit, and others. But after years of assimilationist policies under occasional Chinese rule, Vietnamese scribes created chữ nôm, a writing system inspired by, but not identical to, Chinese. This script lived alongside Classical Chinese, and was used for poetry and prose, where capturing the spoken qualities of Vietnamese was vital. It was in use from the 13th century AD until the French colonial occupation in the 20th century. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_XERXES_ALT_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_5", "en_US", "Xerxes' early brush with destiny came when Darius, preparing to leave for Egypt to quash its rebellion, fell ill and unexpectedly died. In the wake of this death, the question of succession loomed. Should the crown go to Darius’ eldest? Or to Xerxes, the grandson of Cyrus? A debate was arranged to solve this question, and Xerxes won, taking the throne in 486 BC, at the age of 35. He also inherited his father’s wars and immediately took to quelling Egypt, followed by Babylon after that city rebelled. He enacted a brutal vengeance upon Babylon, melting the statues of its gods in punishment. Indeed, Xerxes was staunchly religious (likely adhering to a precursor of Zoroastrianism)."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_GAUL_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_4", "en_US", "By the fourth century, the Gauls' population expanded into areas bordering the Etruscans and Romans in what is now northern Italy. Though these groups had previous peaceful contact via trade, war broke out, both between Gauls and these civilizations as well as between different Gallic tribes, as the need for resources and land grew with the population. In 387 BC, the Gauls sacked Rome under the leadership of Brennus, chieftain of the Senones, a Gallic tribe, establishing their status as the primary threat to Rome at that time. Skirmishes over the next decades in Italy resulted in a peace between Gaul and Rome that lasted for a few decades. Though the sack of Rome had no lasting negative consequences for Rome's power, it had a profound psychological effect, leading Romans to consider Gauls fearsome for centuries."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_GAUL_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_6", "en_US", "By the late first century BC, Gaul’s internal conflicts provided ample opportunity for Roman intervention. When northern tribes threatened the southern Gallic commerce centers, Rome came to their aid, but not with the intent of defending their independence. Rather, Rome used this opportunity for its own expansion, fully annexing Transalpine Gaul (modern Provence) in 121 BC. In 113, the Cimbrian War broke out as the Cimbri and other Gallo-Germanic groups tried to migrate into new lands. They successfully defeated Rome in a series of battles, but were slow in their advance, allowing the Romans to recover. In 101, the Cimbri moved south through the Alps, but the superior Roman forces proved victorious. The Cimbri were decimated, their leaders killed, their women and children choosing suicide over slavery."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_GAUL_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_7", "en_US", "From Provence, Rome had a base to advance further military actions, and it had an economic hold over the ports along the Mediterranean as well as the riverways leading north. From here, Julius Caesar decided to move north and conquer the rest of Gaul, nominally due to the migration of various Gallic tribes, but more likely due to his career ambitions and rising debts. Initially, Gallic tribes put up a strong fight, even defeating Caesar, yet by 56 BC, Julius had turned the tables and defeated the Veneti (a tribe of seafarers from modern Brittany), taking much of northwestern Gaul. The Gauls reacted strongly. For the first time, a Gallic chieftain, Vercingetorix, began uniting the tribes in an effort to hold their land against the Roman invaders. Yet, despite a series of Gallic victories, the Romans eventually were victorious. Caesar went home to deal with Rome's civil wars, but Gaul would never reclaim her independence. Revolts continued into the first century AD, but the Roman conquest of Gaul was final."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_GAUL_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_8", "en_US", "The Roman conquest severely impacted the Gallic population. It is estimated that 1 in 12 Gauls were killed and the same number enslaved. Some tribes were completely eliminated and entire cities slaughtered. As Gaul settled disquietly into a Roman province, old oppida became Roman cities, and eventually Gauls were granted Roman citizenship in 212 AD. The hybridized Gallo-Roman language and culture further developed, eventually mixing with Frankish and other cultures in the urban centers of the Loire Valley during the early medieval period. Even today, Gallic culture remains an element of identity in modern France."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_VIX_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "The grave dates back to around 500 BC and contained the remains of a woman along with many ornate pieces of jewelry. She was buried in a chariot along with many grave goods, a funerary custom practiced by several Gallic tribes. Her demonstrably high status has led many to refer to her as the Lady of Vix, though her historical identity remains a mystery. It is possible she was a priestess or member of an elite merchant family as the highest social group at this time seems to have been based on trade."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_GAUL_VAE_VICTIS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In 387 BC, an army of Senones, a Gallic tribe, under the command of their chieftain Brennus, sacked Rome in what is remembered as the first instance of Rome falling to outsiders. The Senones had been pressing into northern Italy in search of additional settlement land, feeling the pressures of expansion and population growth. At peace talks with Rome, a Roman ambassador killed one of the Gallic chieftains, an act that betrayed the common practice of treating ambassadors as neutral. When Rome chose to not punish the offender, the Gauls were greatly offended and decided to attack Rome. After winning the Battle of Allia about ten miles north of Rome, they marched on the city, burning buildings and indiscriminately killing its inhabitants."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_GAISATAI_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Gaisatai literally means “those with spears,” and thus likely supports the common theory that the Gaisatai were bands of Gallic mercenaries who hired themselves out to other Gallic tribes, typically in altercations with Rome. Like most Gallic warriors, they carried a long spear, an oblong shield, and a heavy longsword for close combat. In 225 BC at the Battle of Telamon, the Gaisatai and other Gallic forces stood firm against heavy Roman infantry but eventually fell to Rome’s superior cavalry. The Gaisatai were slaughtered or captured, their leader Aneroëstes and several followers committed suicide, and Rome went on to settle the formerly Celtic lands of Northern Italy."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_GAISATAI_2_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Gaisatai literally means “those with spears,” and thus likely supports the common theory that the Gaisatai were bands of Gallic mercenaries who hired themselves out to other Gallic tribes, typically in altercations with Rome. Like most Gallic warriors, they carried a long spear, an oblong shield, and a heavy longsword for close combat. In 225 BC at the Battle of Telamon, the Gaisatai and other Gallic forces stood firm against heavy Roman infantry but eventually fell to Rome’s superior cavalry. The Gaisatai were slaughtered or captured, their leader Aneroëstes and several followers committed suicide, and Rome went on to settle the formerly Celtic lands of Northern Italy."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_GAISATAI_3_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Gaisatai literally means “those with spears,” and thus likely supports the common theory that the Gaisatai were bands of Gallic mercenaries who hired themselves out to other Gallic tribes, typically in altercations with Rome. Like most Gallic warriors, they carried a long spear, an oblong shield, and a heavy longsword for close combat. In 225 BC at the Battle of Telamon, the Gaisatai and other Gallic forces stood firm against heavy Roman infantry but eventually fell to Rome’s superior cavalry. The Gaisatai were slaughtered or captured, their leader Aneroëstes and several followers committed suicide, and Rome went on to settle the formerly Celtic lands of Northern Italy."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_GILGAMESH_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_5", "en_US", "We know the Epic through a series of tablets, dating from the Sumerian period to the Babylonian and later Akkadian periods – it is the Akkadian Gilgamesh presented here. The most significant find was the discovery in 1853 AD of the Epic in the library of the seventh century BC Assyrian king Ashurbanipal. That Ashurbanipal preserved the Epic is a testament to the staying power of the story: Ashurbanipal’s version was written over a thousand years after the earliest surviving copy."),
("LOC_PEDIA_LEADERS_PAGE_LEADER_GILGAMESH_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_6", "en_US", "Was Gilgamesh real? Sumerian king lists do mention a Gilgamesh of Uruk around 2900 BC, but little other connection exists between the heroic Gilgamesh and any historical figure. Uruk at the time was a city-state in present-day Iraq, and ruled over an increasingly restive land near the end of its height. It would have been a fortress of urban life in a very different world, one with few cities and vast stretches outside of state control – wild men and forest-monsters, indeed. Nonetheless, its voice still calls to us, telling us that humanity’s time on earth is brief, and even the strongest among us fall. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_GORYEO_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Goryeo was the predominant Korean state from the 10th century AD until the end of the 14th and gives us the name “Korea” today. It incorporated Buddhism and Confucianism into broader Korean society, advanced Korean arts and sciences, and stood at a crucial crossroads in the power struggles that shaped East Asia."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_GORYEO_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "In the first millennium AD, the “three kingdoms” of Silla, Baekje, and Goguryeo were shakily unified under Silla, a regime that divided its people up into rigid systems of categorization – by region, but also by its “bone-rank” system of nobility. As Silla devolved into inter-noble fighting, a young prince of the kingdom, to be known later as Kung Ye, was born marked by a prophecy that he would spell disaster. Kung Ye, having grown up sentenced to death but kept alive in hiding, launched a campaign against unstable Silla and declared Goguryeo’s independence."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_TOBANG_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the later part of the Goryeo period (after 1170 AD), the military assumed near-direct control, keeping a king as a figurehead over what was in practice a military state. This was in some part a reaction against the accumulation of perceived slights towards military figures in Goryeo society, as well as the perceived threat from the Mongols and Mongol-dominated Yuan China so near to the Korean border. This regime held its power via the tobang – military personnel tasked with policing civil officials and ensuring loyalty to the military (and its ruling Choe family). This militarized society successfully defended Korea against invasions by the Khitans and Yuan alike. The era of the tobang fell as royal elites brokered a diplomatic solution, ending the threat of a Chinese invasion and resulting in the subjugation of Goryeo as a vassal state to the Yuan."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_TOBANG_2_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the later part of the Goryeo period (after 1170 AD), the military assumed near-direct control, keeping a king as a figurehead over what was in practice a military state. This was in some part a reaction against the accumulation of perceived slights towards military figures in Goryeo society, as well as the perceived threat from the Mongols and Mongol-dominated Yuan China so near to the Korean border. This regime held its power via the tobang – military personnel tasked with policing civil officials and ensuring loyalty to the military (and its ruling Choe family). This militarized society successfully defended Korea against invasions by the Khitans and Yuan alike. The era of the tobang fell as royal elites brokered a diplomatic solution, ending the threat of a Chinese invasion and resulting in the subjugation of Goryeo as a vassal state to the Yuan."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_TOBANG_3_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the later part of the Goryeo period (after 1170 AD), the military assumed near-direct control, keeping a king as a figurehead over what was in practice a military state. This was in some part a reaction against the accumulation of perceived slights towards military figures in Goryeo society, as well as the perceived threat from the Mongols and Mongol-dominated Yuan China so near to the Korean border. This regime held its power via the tobang – military personnel tasked with policing civil officials and ensuring loyalty to the military (and its ruling Choe family). This militarized society successfully defended Korea against invasions by the Khitans and Yuan alike. The era of the tobang fell as royal elites brokered a diplomatic solution, ending the threat of a Chinese invasion and resulting in the subjugation of Goryeo as a vassal state to the Yuan."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_HEIAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The first flowerings of a recognizable Japan emerge with the introduction of Buddhism and the Chinese script in 538 AD. But it is the Heian era (794-1185 AD) where recognizably Japanese arts and literature shine. From the meticulously laid-out city of Heian-kyo (present-day Kyoto) grew an era of relative calm, so much so that its very name – Heian – means “peace.” The capital was a place of poetry, of refinement, of courtly life and intrigue."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_HEIAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "Prior to Heian, Japan had deep cultural links with China, seeing the Chinese Tang Dynasty as a source of Buddhist knowledge and literary culture. But as Tang faced growing instability and eventual collapse in 907 AD, these links were severed and Heian, left on its own, moved forward its own cultural and religious innovations."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_HEIAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_7", "en_US", "Out of conflict between the refined city and martial country, powerful clans arose. The most influential of these was the Heian-kyo (Kyoto)-based Fujiwara. Fujiwara was deeply entwined with the central court, as lords sent their daughters to the imperial palace and exerted a strong influence upon the emperors that emerged from this line. But this system of informal control was to break: Emperor Go-Sanjo, one of the first emperors in centuries to not have a Fujiwara mother, made it his mission to purge the Fujiwara from their positions of influence. The Fujiwara made a last, desperate bid for power in the Hogen Rebellion of 1156 AD, but were crushed. But if Go-Sanjo sought independence, he did not find it: out of this war came not a new period of direct imperial control free from clan influence, but a substitution of Fujiwara puppet masters for another clan, the Taira."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_HEIAN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_8", "en_US", "Taira machinations were also to end in tragedy as other clans, seeing the fall of Fujiwara, sought to try their hand – notably, in the Genpei War between the Kyoto-based Taira and the rising Minamoto clan, whose power base was in the countryside. In the Battle of Dan-no-ura, on the Shimonoseki Strait in 1185 AD, the Taira fleet was destroyed. According to legend, the Taira families, watching the battle from the shores, flung themselves into the sea rather than face defeat. The victorious Minamoto were to relocate the base of practical power to Kamakura, though the court and capital remained Kyoto. This configuration – a distant military capital protecting an inward-looking court – created a new era of power and a new model of rule: the shogunate."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_HOO_DO_HALL_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Originally built as a manor for a Minamoto lord in 998 AD, Byodo-in became a temple under the Fujiwara clan in 1052 AD. In the year following, the temple constructed a dramatic hall, with sweeping outstretched wings enfolding an artificial lake. The structure resembled a phoenix – hoo, in Japanese – and the hall is now known as “Hoo-do,” or the Phoenix Hall. Gracing its top is a golden statue of the hoo, gazing down upon the majesty of the place."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_HEIAN_URBAN_GRID_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Heian-kyo – modern-day Kyoto – was to be a model for a new Japan. Built by the Emperor Kammu in 794 AD, Heian-kyo was modeled after the Tang capital at Chang’an, and like that city, was built on an urban grid. This grid was not just aesthetically pleasing; it was a model for society. The palace sat at the northern end of the city and the rest  was divided into boulevards separating areas for commerce, administration, residence, and urban villas. The city also followed the principles of geomancy – a properly-laid-out town would ensure prosperity for the state. Yet the grid proved too ambitious: the outskirts of the capital had gone to farmland by the end of the period."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_SHIJIN_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "What emerged from the following isolation was a flowering of literature. Japanese writing began to incorporate the phonetic kana (hiragana and katakana) that enabled expression in Japanese language. Writing styles focused on melancholic themes – love and love’s ending, the fading of summer into autumn, or the appeal of ruins – and took inspiration from the image of the cloud-shrouded moon. “Poem tales,” novelistic works of prose, became particularly popular, especially among female writers, and Murasaki Shikibu’s Tale of Genji, written around 1010 AD, ranks supreme among these: a tale of an imperial son of a low-ranking concubine who, stripped of his position, spends his time in love affairs amidst the splendor of a court whose highest levels have been denied him."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_ICELAND_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In 874 AD, Ingólfr Arnarson gathered his household and set sail from Norway to an uninhabited island in the middle of the North Atlantic Ocean. The land had been discovered some years earlier, first named Snæland (Snow Land), then Garðarshólmi (Garðr’s Island, by a man named Garðr), and finally Ísland (Ice Land) after its immense western glacier. As Ingólfr’s longship approached the coast, he brought out the pillars that accompanied his hall’s high seat and threw them into the sea. Guided by the currents, fate, and perhaps the gods, they drifted ashore near what is today Reykjavík, where Ingólfr built his farm. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_EX_ICELAND_VAERINGJAR_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Norse warriors did not exclusively operate as Viking raiders – they were also highly valued as mercenaries across Europe and beyond. The most famous of these were the Væringjar, members of the Varangian Guard who served the Byzantine Emperors during the 10th and 11th centuries. The unit was founded in 988 AD by Emperor Basil II out of 2,000 Norse and Slavic warriors sent by Prince Vladimir I of Kiev. They were highly regarded as great warriors, often placed in the vanguard during major conflicts and entrusted as the emperor’s bodyguards. The most famous Varangian was Haraldr Sigurðarson, nicknamed harðráði, who later became king of Norway. The only Norseman named in Byzantine sources, he was deployed against the Bulgars in the Balkans and the Arabs in Sicily before returning north to claim his throne. By the mid-11th century, the Norse contingent of the Guard began to shrink, increasingly replaced by Frankish and English warriors until the unit dissolved during the fall of Constantinople in 1204."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_NIRAYAMA_REVERBERATORY_FURNACE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Built off of Dutch models, the furnace was completed in 1855-1857 AD. Its design involves keeping the furnace – beneath the two iconic smokestacks – separate from the iron, allowing the metal to melt without the introduction of by-products from the forge. Molten metal would then pour into molds in a central pit – molds, especially, of cannons. A Japan that was to defend against the kurofune would need its naval armaments."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_MIREUKSA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Buddhism came to Baekje in 385 AD and, like elsewhere in the region, became closely associated with the power of the monarchy. Visiting a lake in the southern part of Baekje, King Mu received a vision of the Maitreya Buddha (the Buddha who is to come). Mu had the water drained and constructed on the site a temple complex including two stone pagodas and a massive wooden building linked by a covered hall. Gold plaques professing the monarchs’ devotion to Buddhism were installed at the site, though the original woodworking has long since disappeared."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_JOSEON_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Korea’s last major dynasty, the kingdom of Joseon (1392-1897 AD) saw the House of Yi ruling the peninsula (albeit as a tributary state of Ming and Qing) for over five hundred years. Described as “the hermit kingdom” owing to its isolation and self-reliance, Joseon was a point of stability through the high era of colonialism, a bastion of Confucianism, and a difficult target for would-be invaders. In a world that seemed to be crumbling around them, Koreans in Joseon sought a firm footing – and a path forward."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_NEPAL_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "On the northern frontier of India, and in amongst the high mountain valleys, Nepalese kingdoms existed as tributary states since the fist millennium AD, where the name “Nepala” flickers in an out of the inscriptions of states such as the Guptas. They emerge in force in the 10th century AD, with the founding of Kathmandu, but it is not until the 18th century that a significant power arises – the Gorkha Dynasty, and its founder, Prithvi Narayan Shah."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_JANISSARY_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the 14th century, the Sultan of the Ottoman Empire, either Murad I or his father Orhan, chose to create a personal army of men loyal only to him. Initially, these Janissaries, as they were called, were recruited from non-Muslim slaves, primarily from the Balkans. The Ottomans would choose young children from these groups, place them with Turkish families to convert to Islam and learn Turkish, and then enroll them in the Janissary Corps once deemed mature and fit.  Jannissaries were one of the first infantry armies to equip firearms, and their excellent training and loyalty made them a fearsome force throughout the 14th through 17th centuries. They were instrumental in all of the major Ottoman campaigns, including the capture of Constantinople in 1453 AD. As the primary infantry force within the Ottoman military, they played a key role in protecting the core of the force, especially the Sultan, while the elite Turkish cavalry charged and feinted from the flanks."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_JANISSARY_2_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the 14th century, the Sultan of the Ottoman Empire, either Murad I or his father Orhan, chose to create a personal army of men loyal only to him. Initially, these Janissaries, as they were called, were recruited from non-Muslim slaves, primarily from the Balkans. The Ottomans would choose young children from these groups, place them with Turkish families to convert to Islam and learn Turkish, and then enroll them in the Janissary Corps once deemed mature and fit.  Jannissaries were one of the first infantry armies to equip firearms, and their excellent training and loyalty made them a fearsome force throughout the 14th through 17th centuries. They were instrumental in all of the major Ottoman campaigns, including the capture of Constantinople in 1453 AD. As the primary infantry force within the Ottoman military, they played a key role in protecting the core of the force, especially the Sultan, while the elite Turkish cavalry charged and feinted from the flanks."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_UNIT_JANISSARY_3_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the 14th century, the Sultan of the Ottoman Empire, either Murad I or his father Orhan, chose to create a personal army of men loyal only to him. Initially, these Janissaries, as they were called, were recruited from non-Muslim slaves, primarily from the Balkans. The Ottomans would choose young children from these groups, place them with Turkish families to convert to Islam and learn Turkish, and then enroll them in the Janissary Corps once deemed mature and fit.  Jannissaries were one of the first infantry armies to equip firearms, and their excellent training and loyalty made them a fearsome force throughout the 14th through 17th centuries. They were instrumental in all of the major Ottoman campaigns, including the capture of Constantinople in 1453 AD. As the primary infantry force within the Ottoman military, they played a key role in protecting the core of the force, especially the Sultan, while the elite Turkish cavalry charged and feinted from the flanks."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_SENGOKU_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "In the “floating world” of the Edo period (the Tokugawa shogunate, 1603 AD – 1868 AD) of Japan, in the taverns and gathering places where samurai spent time after a day of bureaucratic administration, they told stories of other samurai: armored warriors sweeping across a field of battle, daring escapes, cunning ninja, and heroic leaders. These were the stories that shaped our impression of feudal Japan; these were the stories of the Sengoku Jidai, the time of war. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_SENGOKU_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "The Sengoku period, beginning in the late 15th century AD and ending near the start of the 17th, was a time, too, of countless possibilities for Japan’s future. Even as feudal clans struggled for control over the imperial palace, Christianity began to make inroads in the south, gunpowder took to the field, lordless peasant republics flickered in and out of existence. At its end, the chaos was put to rest with the ascendance of a powerful shogunate, the establishment of the prosperous Edo period…and the closing of Japan to the world."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_SENGOKU_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "“Sengoku” means “the time of the country at war,” and as such does not correspond with any of the traditional divisions of Japanese history, which are normally named for the dynasty in the imperial palace at the time. Formally, most of Sengoku was during the Muromachi or Ashikaga period (1336 AD – 1573 AD), named for the shogunate ruling at the time, though the first part of this era did not devolve into the chaos – that which we call “Sengoku” marks a period of instability out of which the legends of feudal Japan were born."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_SHAWNEE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The origins of the Shawnee tribe are murky, but they are widely believed to have emerged from the Fort Ancient culture of the Ohio River Valley (c. 1000-1650 AD). The Fort Ancient people emerged from the Late Woodland period with a new understanding of corn agriculture and farming techniques, leading to a more sedentary lifestyle and larger settlements. They did not form a single polity; rather, they shared a common culture of social organization, religious beliefs, artistic styles, and mound-building that spanned across diverse political and linguistic groups. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_SERPENT_MOUND_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "On a plateau in modern-day Ohio, at the site of an ancient crater, indigenous people erected a winding mound in the shape of a great serpent. The site was likely selected because of its unusual terrain and was built up by a variety of different peoples over the course of more than a thousand years, between 320 BC and 1400 AD. The mound coils across the land, settling into the landscape, and the mouth of the serpent appears to consume an oval object, perhaps an egg (possibly once the site of a temple)."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_SILLA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "The Three Kingdoms of Korea were a set of culturally similar states on the peninsula in the first millennium AD. Of these, Silla was both the oldest and the most distant from Chinese influence, but each of the Three Kingdoms shared a militarized society and centralized system of government with a powerful monarch and aristocracy. In the early years of the Three Kingdoms, each power sought to balance the others through selective alliances and well-timed advances. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_SILLA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_6", "en_US", "But there were cracks forming. The suppression of local nobles could work in a truly isolated place, but when these nobles began to grow rich on trade, they gained power that they used to challenge the central state. Riven by civil war, in the 10th century AD, Silla surrendered to the state of Goryeo. "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_QUARTER_DRAGON_TEMPLE_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Buddhism arrived in Korea around the 4th century AD, and became a significant political tool – and point of contention – for Silla. Korean animism, focused around the worship of clan deities, had been central to the cultivation of elite power. But powerful noble families are an impediment to a centralized monarchy, which seeks the supremacy of one family over all – thus, monarchs of Silla adopted Buddhist religion, and sponsored the construction of massive temples – sachal, in Korean - within the capital."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_TONGA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "Tonga’s first inhabitants landed on its shores around 3200 years ago, c. 1000 BC. These were the Lapita people, originally from Southeast Asia, talented navigators who rapidly spread across the Pacific. Their presence is marked largely by the remains of their distinctive pottery, mostly cooking and storage containers, featuring ornate repeating geometric designs. However, within two centuries they had ceased to decorate their pottery – the reason for this sudden shift is unknown."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_TONGA_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_3", "en_US", "The subsequent Plainware Period (700 BC-400 AD) saw the transition to a permanent Polynesian society in Tonga. While the first generations of Lapita settlers lived along the coast and relied almost entirely on the ocean for food and livelihoods, the Plainware people moved inland, establishing farms and plantations and raising livestock. But around 400 AD, another unexplained shift occurred – Tongans stopped producing pottery altogether, again for unknown reasons. The absence of pottery and other archaeological evidence lasted for several centuries, and very little is known about what occurred during this Dark Age. However, society persisted, continuing to develop and grow, and around 950 AD, the Tu'i Tonga clan started to consolidate power on the island of Tongatapu."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_TONGA_LAPITA_ORIGINS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Archaeological evidence shows signs of the Tongan islands being colonized no later than 1000 BC. The early settlers were part of a community known as the Lapita cultural complex, a group of Neolithic Austronesian peoples who settled much of Oceania. They developed a robust system of maritime trade across Polynesia, trading their unique style of pottery marked by a signature geometric pattern. They primarily settled on coastlines though over time, we see settlements move inland and agriculture become more important."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_NODE_CIVIC_AQ_TONGA_LAPITA_ORIGINS_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_2", "en_US", "A few hundred years later their unique pottery had disappeared. Though the Tongan islands remained inhabited, pottery production ceased completely by 400 AD for unknown reasons and never returned. Later, during the time of the Tongan maritime empire, a new form of tradecraft developed – ngatu, decorated bark cloth, also known as “tapa cloth,” made from the mulberry tree. Pieces of bark are flattened with a mallet and joined together to create a fabric. In the past, ngatu was used for clothing as well as ceremonial purposes, with the latter being beautifully decorated with imagery of fauna and flora. Today, Tongans use ngatu in weddings, funerals, and other important cultural events."),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_WONDER_NAN_MADOL_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_1", "en_US", "Some time in the late 12th century AD the Saudeleur Dynasty arose in the Pohnpei islands of what is now Micronesia. At its capital was a city of canals and artificial islands, built with large slabs of basalt and coral, and interwoven with tombs and residences. Many of these islands were specialized toward particular activities – the construction of canoes, for instance, or the housing of the families of distant chiefs (the better to control their homelands). "),
("LOC_PEDIA_PAGE_CIVILIZATION_AKSUM_CHAPTER_HISTORY_PARA_4", "en_US", "Aksum’s days were numbered as Muslim caliphates rose in the seventh century CE. The expanding Arab powers not only threatened Aksumite possessions in Yemen, but the Muslim conquest of Egypt and the establishment of trade empires based on religious connections meant that Aksum’s culture-defining trade routes went nowhere under Ethiopian rule. Unwilling to convert this time, the Aksumite kings faded into the introverted Ethiopian dynasties of the medieval period.");
